# Design spec — decisions before generation

Applies to every change that alters what a user-visible surface renders —
`app/ui/` and `app/ui_admin/` source (.js/.css/.html/.j2,
excluding generated bundles). The mechanical layer records when the first
UI edit lands and when the spec was written; a spec written *after* the
first edit fails the gate. This rule is why the gate exists — the hook only
checks the artifact's shape.

## Why this rule exists (the failure it kills)

The quality gap between surfaces isn't a capability gap — it is a
conditioning gap. Left to itself, generation composes a surface
monolithically in one pass, conditioned on the request's literal words and
the corpus prior ("what channel pages look like generally"), and lands at
the median: functional, flat, utility-grade. The designed surfaces in this
repo (the discussion board is the bar) were built the other way: a concept
first, a grammar second, element decisions third, code last — hundreds of
adjudicated micro-calls, each given local attention, none left to be made
silently mid-code.

The remedy is structural: **move the decisions out of generation
entirely.** Every call that determines what the surface looks like gets
made in a written, inspectable spec *before* the first edit. When the spec
is complete, the code is a transcription of decisions — and transcription
carries almost no variance. A first pass stops being a sample and becomes
the spec itself rendered.

## The artifact — required before the first UI edit

Path: `.unlazy/design-spec/<session_id>.md` (the hook prints the path when
a UI edit lands without one). A spec may instead live under a work-unit
name (`gamersocial-v2.md`) — it then MUST declare `session: <session_id>`
(or `OWNS: <session_id>`) within its first ~40 lines so the gate can
resolve it to the session. Specs created this session are v3: a v1/v2
marker on a newly-authored spec fails DESIGN-SPEC outright (a pre-existing
v2 spec only edited still parses with a note).

Shape:

```
<!-- design-spec v3 -->   (v1/v2 docs still parse with an upgrade note —
new work writes v3; the marker claims the v2 machinery below plus the
v3 additions — register axis on Directions, tonal budget, read-axis
bar + bar-floor, audience row, surface center-of-gravity, live risk
register — and the gate checks it)

## Constraint ledger
<every constraint-bearing instruction in the ask AND the carried
thread, quoted VERBATIM — tagged by class: `content` | `method` |
`decision` | `approval`. The source is the whole conversation
history, not only open complaints: approvals and ratified decisions
from earlier iterations are binding constraints that die quietly
because they feel "settled" — a ledger whose rows are all defects
carries criticisms and drops decisions. On a continued or resumed
thread, re-extract constraints from the history/summary BEFORE
composing: each prior instruction becomes a row or an explicit
`dropped — <why>` row, never absent. Method constraints (identical,
standalone, separate, each, sizes, orderings, exclusions) are the
fragile class — the first thing compression drops between the ask
and the plan. EVERY row carries its `check:` form — the falsifiable
test that proves it held ("identical chrome → node/class-level diff
vs the captured real DOM"; "≤70% wide → probe rect vs #gh-main
content box at 3840"; "not the board → toolbar pattern ≠ gh-seg").
A constraint no check can bind is an Open call surfaced to the user,
never absorbed silently; `DECLARED:` rows stay visibly unverified
rather than passing as prose. The review artifact's
## Constraint check diffs the shipped surface against this ledger,
row by row.

v3 — every spec also carries an `audience` row: who actually looks at
this surface and on what display/environment (e.g. "consumer desktop,
creator monitors → matrix must reach ≥3840"; "phone-first commuters
→ 390/768/1440"). The coverage matrix is DERIVED from this row —
1440/768/390 by convention is not an audience. The petnet/gamersocial
failure proved the gap: both matrices topped at 1440 while the user's
monitor is 3840 — the viewport the product actually got judged at was
never in the enumerated universe>
|| class | constraint (quoted) | check: |
||---|---|---|
|| audience | <who looks at this + display/environment — quoted or derived> | <matrix widths that cover it> |

## Exemplar
anchor: <in-repo surface this work is held to> — e.g. the discussion
  board (topics board in-group) — or "none — no house referent"
class: <the artifact kind being built — "a board", "a notice strip",
  "a sheet". This is the surface's POSITIVE referent — what it IS,
  stated before any contrast sentence. "Not X" / "slimmer Y" is
  banned as a definition: a surface defined only by differences
  inherits the sibling's parameter space, which is how a concept
  set collapses to one axis. Name the real exemplar class first
  ("a curated file library — Nexus file section, GitHub Releases"),
  then contrasts may qualify it>
external: <2-3 real exemplars of the class, required when the house
  referent is "none" — or "n/a — house referent exists">
captures: <screenshot paths of the REAL surfaces this work will be
  judged against — the exemplar surface AND the shell chrome it sits
  inside (topbar, sidebar, member rail, logo) — at the target
  viewports, every path resolving to a real file. Grammar from
  source + shots is artifact-level; grammar from memory or a summary
  doc is adjectives. No captures → the spec is not complete and no
  code exists yet. The only legal absence is
  `DECLARED: no captures — <why no real surface of this class can
  be captured>` — and "no house referent" is never that why: a
  public exemplar (Facebook, Steam, Airbnb) is capturable on demand.
  A referent ban is not an inability either — `anchor: none`,
  "the house referent is off-limits", or any direction choice that
  excludes referents leaves the comparative bar unevidenced; the
  declaration only stands when *no surface of the class exists to
  capture at all*. `captures: none in-repo` is memory wearing an
  exemption's name>
craft: <per named exemplar: ≥2 craft-level traits — not anatomy, the
  WHY it reads as premium ("Meetup's card works because the image is a
  photograph OF the event — the payload IS the picture", "Instagram's
  head works because the stats row is one scannable breath") — each
  trait mapped to where it lands in THIS product. Anatomy transfer
  without craft transfer is how "image-top card" became a wallpaper
  band: the slot survived, the payload died. `craft:` rows name the
  payload role of every adopted element — an element whose exemplar
  role was content may not land as decoration>
intent: <required when the request carries a number or soft size word
  ("60%", "sorta narrow", "a bit wider") — restate the intent in
  non-numeric terms ("a narrow focused list like Canny, not 60% of
  a wide column") BEFORE choosing a mechanism. A number in the
  request is a proxy, never the spec>
skills loaded: <the design/taste skills read for this work —
  `ls .devin/skills/` then every design-*/taste-* SKILL.md that
  applies, listed by name. An unloaded skill is an unread rule>

## Chrome
equivalent: <the exemplar's corresponding surface — the thing users
  do the same job on, or "none — no exemplar surface exists">
exemplar-chrome: <its chrome class — page | reader | modal | sheet |
  popover | rail | inline>
chosen: <the chrome class this surface uses, and why — inherited from
  the exemplar, or defended in Open calls>
alternatives: <the classes considered and set aside — chrome is a
  decided axis, never adopted silently from the nearest pattern>
budget: <what the chosen chrome may host — a modal/sheet holds at
  most one scroll column and ~3 sections; a surface that overflows
  that budget is structural evidence for a page, not a styling
  problem to compress>

## Directions
<required on generative asks — a new surface, a mockup, a redesign:
≥2 genuinely divergent directions (not variants of one), each with
the trade-off that makes it real, then the pick and why it beat the
runner-up. Enumerated BEFORE the concept sentence — directions
written after a choice is already made are post-hoc justification
wearing a planning costume. The nearest-anchor failure (cloning the
exemplar because "similar" was said) is what this section exists to
kill — "similar in quality" and "same structure" are different
requests and the table must state which reading was taken.

Each direction declares its AXES — where it diverges: anatomy (rows
vs cards vs detail-forward), emphasis map (what's loudest), density,
grouping, surface model (library vs feed vs store vs launcher) — AND
its CONDITIONING — which referent or referent-blend from ## Exemplar
it draws from (a referent named in the spec's preamble but bound to
no direction is decoration; conditioning is what propagates). A set
of concepts that varies only one axis is ONE concept with a
parameter — "all 3 look identical" is the painted form of that
sample. The set must show ≥2 distinct axes across it, or defend why
the design space is genuinely one-dimensional. The axes must also be
RESOURCED at the format level: a concept set sharing one data schema
and one row renderer can only vary parameters — if the concepts will
diverge in anatomy or surface model, the spec says so here (separate
schemas / separate renderers / separate files), because "three
concepts, one file, one rowHTML" caps divergence at grouping by
construction>
| direction | what it would be | axes it varies | register | conditioned by (referent) | payload allocation (primary / secondary / behind-expansion) | the trade-off | disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <each> | | | | | | | CHOSE / SET-ASIDE: <why> |

Each direction ALSO declares its REGISTER — its position on the
continuous axes the corpus prior always samples at the mean:
muted↔vivid · flat↔layered · sparse↔dense · photo↔illustration ·
quiet↔loud chrome. The mean sample (muted, flat, medium-density,
utility chrome) is what "clean modern UI" defaults to when nobody
decides — a set whose every candidate sits at the mean on every
axis is one sample re-boxed, the register version of the one-axis
flag. Vary register across the set with evidence-of-fit per
candidate ("vivid because the payload is authored media", "layered
because the surface is sparse by design"), or defend why the design
space is genuinely mean-register (a compliance table earns flat).
Register choices are WHERE taste enters the spec — the section
exists because bland shipped under specs that never made the call.

Each direction ALSO declares its PAYLOAD ALLOCATION — which facts
live on the primary surface (row/card line), which are secondary,
and which sit behind expansion. A surface's anatomy IS its
allocation: "index table" and "card list" are different registers
only if the facts they carry in view differ — an index row carries
one line of facts with the rest behind ⌄, a card carries the rich
payload on its face. Identical allocation across every direction is
the one-concept-re-boxed flag — the table that ported the full card
payload into row 1 is what this row exists to kill.

The set also declares its SHARED SCAFFOLD — everything the concepts
do NOT vary: data schema, row/card renderer, anatomy, emphasis map,
grouping, surface model. Convergence you can't see is convergence you
can't check: "all 3 share rowHTML + FILES + one anatomy" written out
loud is the flag "all 3 look identical" arrives too late to be.
A scaffold row that covers every structural dimension is the one-axis
sample confessing itself — and a scaffold row covering payload
allocation confesses "same content model, different box" even when
three different renderers exist.
| shared scaffold | what all concepts carry |
|---|---|
| <schema / renderer / anatomy / emphasis map / payload allocation / ...> | <shared or per-concept — and which> |

On a RESTYLE ask each direction ALSO declares what it changes vs the
CURRENT chassis — the existing layout skeleton, palette family, nav
architecture, density class, and information architecture are the
design's inertia. A direction row that names no change to them
inherits them silently, and three directions that all inherit the
same chassis is one re-skin re-boxed. At least one direction in the
set must move a chassis component, or the set defends in ## Delta why
the chassis is already right (the gap was execution, not composition).
Single-idea execution on an existing surface is how "improve the
design" becomes "same page, re-tinted" — the direction table is where
the redesign finds its structure instead of silently inheriting it.

## Concept
<one governing sentence: what kind of place/thing this surface is —
"a governance rail for a topic community", "a citation strip, not a
message row". Unity is authored here, not hoped for.>

## Quality contract
<required whenever ## Directions exists — a generative ask (new
surface, mockup, redesign) carries its quality bar in writing BEFORE
code, because "reads as a product" is a spec'd requirement, not a
verdict discovered at review. Six named fields:
bar: <the measurable bar — a RANK clause, not class membership. Name
  ≥3 checkable properties the 9/10 version of this surface has that a
  passable one lacks ("every media tile is payload, not decoration",
  "hierarchy survives a 2-second squint", "one element a stranger
  screenshots") PLUS the scoring clause a stranger can grade against
  ("9/10 — picks this over the exemplar on feel alone"). "Reads as an
  app like X" is a membership test achievable at 5/10 — banned as the
  whole bar. v3: the bar ALSO names the stranger's READ — "reads as
  illustration-craft, never clip-art", "reads as a product surface,
  never a settings panel" — the read-axis clause carries the banned
  tell explicitly. Composition properties may support the bar; a bar
  whose every clause is compositional (contrast ratios, grid math)
  and none is a stranger-read is satisfiable at the register it was
  written to prevent — a 5/10 wash can pass every arithmetic clause>
bar-floor: <the declared minimum score this direction may land —
  "8/10". Numeric, not adjectival: bar-floor is what makes
  `ceiling < bar-floor` a computable inequality the gate can check,
  and what the verdict-vs-shipped check holds shipped scores against.
  A spec that will accept anything above passable has no bar-floor —
  write the honest number, because the floor the author won't name
  is the floor the reviewer can't hold>
ceiling: <the chosen direction's best-possible outcome, scored
  honestly BEFORE build — "the best execution of this direction lands
  ~7/10 because the media strategy is decoration" is a spec sent back,
  not a spec built. If the defended ceiling is below the bar-floor,
  pick a different direction — the contract exists to catch a
  ceiling-limiting decision while it is still a row in a table>
payload: <one row per media-bearing surface: what the media IS and why
  a stranger reads it as content, not placeholder — "post media is a
  composed scene with depth + focal subject, not a flat icon on a tile";
  "group header is a photo of the activity, or its authored equivalent
  carrying the same information". Decoration-in-the-content-slot is the
  single most common ceiling-killer — every media slot gets this row
  or `DECLARED: no media — <why>`. v3: each payload row ALSO states
  its media REGISTER (photo | illustration | data-scene | abstract) +
  the thumbnail-scale read evidence — what a stranger sees in the
  tile at 40px before parsing anything. A payload register that
  diverges from the exemplar's register (the exemplar's tiles are
  photographs; ours are vector scenes) is a direction-level taste
  risk — it lands in ## Open calls flagged as such, because adopting
  the exemplar's anatomy while overriding its load-bearing register
  is exactly how clip-art shipped beside "payload" rows>
distinctive moves: <≥2 authored moves that break the template read —
  the decisions that make this recognizable as THIS product rather
  than "the exemplar, reskinned" — each with the mechanism that earns
  it ("the paw-print react is the brand mark doing interaction work",
  "the composer speaks pet-voice in placeholder AND chips")>
generic failure modes: <≥3 ways THIS artifact class reads generic —
  enumerated before code ("uniform card rhythm", "media tiles as
  illustration not payload", "flat muted hierarchy") — each paired
  with the designed-out-by mechanism it maps to in the inventory.
  These are the class's failure modes, authored — not the global
  banned-moves list re-pasted>
|| move | mechanism that earns it |
|| mode | designed out by |

A quality contract that only re-states the lazy-signals table is the
same checklist in a second costume — the contract names what makes
it EXCELLENT, lazy signals name what makes it lazy; both exist
because passable and good are different targets.>

## Member questions
<the questions a member brings, in the member's priority order —
"what do I need → what's new → is this right for me → can I trust
it". For a multi-surface build this table is the PRODUCT's question
space; each page's own question-space lives in its `### <surface>`
derivation block (## Surfaces), and the two are not interchangeable:
"who can I play with?" is a product question; "can I narrow this
list?" is a surface question — only the second one generates a
page's affordances. These are a CONTRACT, not notes: the review
artifact's ## Question verdicts re-asks each against the shipped
surface, and an unanswered question is a finding by construction.
Deriving features from these questions is the only license to
include them; "the last surface had it" is not a question>
|| question | member priority | answered by (element, if known) |
||---|---|---|

## Domain checklist
<the artifact class's canonical feature set — derived as the UNION
of ≥2 named real exemplars' actual feature lists, NOT inherited from
the last surface built and NOT recalled from memory. A file library:
format, size, version, download count, detail view, requirements,
uploader, changelog, trust signal, update cadence (Nexus rows carry X;
GitHub Releases carries Y — name them). A league table: fixtures,
standings, form, venue, squad. Each feature gets `included —
<element>` or `omitted — <defended reason>`. An omission with no
defense is a finding; a checklist citing no exemplar sources is
memory wearing derivation's name — fails on its face. Each row also
carries the element's ROLE in the exemplar — payload (it IS the
content), structural (it organizes), chrome (it frames), decoration —
because a feature adopted without its role is the motif-band failure:
the slot arrives, the job it did never does>
One row is mandatory on generative asks (v3 — D15): the declared
minimum PRIMARY-UNITS-PER-DESKTOP-VIEWPORT for this surface class
("a feed: ≥6 content units at 1440"; "a board: ≥20 rows"). An
information-sparse surface at desktop scale is the void-island
failure in miniature — the density probe holds the shipped DOM
against the declared floor.
|| canonical feature | exemplar source | role | disposition |
||---|---|---|

## Embedded surfaces
<every element that itself contains interactions — modal, sheet,
menu, drawer, popover, form, collection list — is its OWN design
unit, not a part of the parent surface. Each re-runs the method:
kind + named exemplar(s) + a canonical-features checklist (a
## Domain checklist block keyed to the surface) with per-feature
`included — <element>` or `omitted — <defended reason>`. A downloads
manager: item identity + icon/preview, source context, version,
date/status, per-item actions (open detail, re-download, remove),
list actions (scope, clear, search), empty state — derived from
real exemplars (browser Downloads, Steam), not assembled ad hoc.
The parent surface's checklist covers the surface; the embedded
checklist covers the interior. Naming a referent without extracting
its canonical features counts as no referent — "browser dl-history
pattern" is a citation, not a derivation. A modal whose interior
has no exemplar and no feature list was plotted, not designed>

Each embedded-surface row MUST also carry, beyond the checklist link:

1. **Anatomy citations.** Checklist rows cite the exemplar's OBSERVED
   anatomy — the element or behavior actually seen in it, with where —
   and the where is a TOKEN, not a name: a capture file, a DOM detail,
   a `file:line` of the real implementation, a URL read. "browser
   Downloads groups items by day + status — dl-history.png" is a
   derivation; "FB post header row" alone is a guess wearing a
   citation. A row that names an exemplar without naming where it was
   observed is memory, not evidence. "file managers" as an exemplar
   is not evidence.
2. **Quality bar.** One sentence naming what the exemplar class would
   NEVER ship — the minimum the surface may not fall below ("a folder
   card that reads as a plain list row", "a report form that is one
   bare textarea"). If a bar cannot be written, the exemplar was not
   inspected.
3. **Anatomy floor.** Any surface with ≥3 interaction points or ≥2
   visual regions populates or defends each zone: identity/header ·
   content · actions · states (hover/focus/empty/loading). A bare
   zone is a defect, not restraint.
4. **Nesting inherits.** A surface that CONTAINS another interactive
   surface (sheet hosting a nested editor, page with inline composers,
   card whose ⋯ opens a rich modal) re-runs this protocol per nested
   unit — the child gets its own row and its own checklist, never a
   mention folded into the parent's row. Recursion is recursive.
5. **Underdesigned test (post-build, before capture).** Compare the
   rendered anatomy density against the exemplar class; if the surface
   is materially thinner than anything its exemplars ship, re-derive —
   do not capture and call it done. `09-design-review.mdc` runs this
   lens during critique.

| surface | kind | exemplar(s) | quality bar | canonical-features checklist location |
|---|---|---|---|---|

## Layout budget
<required for any layout with fixed allocation — colgroup, grid
tracks, px/flex-basis columns, a constrained content measure.
State the arithmetic as a declared inequality PER BREAKPOINT in
the capture matrix: fixed terms + minimum content term = unit
minimum ≤ available width (with the padding/math that produces
each number, not estimates). Include the content-measure equation
where a width constraint applies ("~70% of #gh-main at 3840 →
≤2688px"), every fixed column's usable interior vs the largest
control it must hold, and the responsive shedding plan — which
term yields at which width. An edit inside a shared budget must
RESTATE the whole equation, not the touched line: subtracting
from one column without recomputing the row is how a 100px
action cell shipped holding a 100px button minus 20px padding.
The probe verifies declared math against rendered geometry —
unstated budget = unverified layout>
| breakpoint | equation | min unit width | available width | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|

## Tonal budget
<required whenever ## Directions exists on a chrome-bearing surface 
(v3 — the arithmetic answer to "washed out"): a declared luminance 
inequality across the chrome stack, authored like the layout budget 
so the arithmetic probe can verify it. Both failed products lived in 
a ~3% relative-luminance band (#F6F1E8→#FFFCF7→#E3D9C7 is arithmetic, 
not a vibe) — flatness you can compute is flatness you can gate. 
Each level declares its token + expected relative luminance; the 
declared ΔL floor is the minimum separation a stranger can see 
(bg→surface ≥ ~2%, surface→raised ≥ ~3%, or a defended equivalent — 
dark themes declare the same stack inverted). Deliberate low-contrast 
brands exempt per pair with `DECLARED: tonal — <pair> <why>`.
Media/payload regions are exempt — the budget binds CHROME (surfaces, 
rails, cards, dividers), where washed-out ships>
|| level | token | declared L (rel) | pair floor ΔL |
||---|---|---|---|
|| <bg / surface / raised / line / accent-mute> | <var(--…)> | <0.0–1.0> | <min separation vs the adjacent level> |

The budget also declares `accent-budget: <N>` — the maximum number of
elements per surface the accent hue may paint (color, background, or
border within tolerance of the accent token). A docs page whose accent
lives on ~1,400 code pills has no accent — it has a hue tax, and the
spread is arithmetic the coverage probe now measures. Measured over
declared flags ACCENT-SPREAD; `DECLARED: accent — <why>` dispositions a
deliberate accent-heavy surface.

## Emphasis ladder
<every element on the surface ranked 1–5 — the loudest thing a user
should see is rank 1 — with the treatment each rank buys (size /
weight / saturation / position / prominence). The member's primary
action is rank 1 or the spec defends why not. Rank determines
treatment, never vice versa — writing the ladder BEFORE the
inventory is what makes emphasis a decision instead of a default.
Rank-1 and rank-2 treatments must cite the exemplar's treatment — a
real referent supplies the shape ("Download = the filled accent CTA
this artifact class uses"), because unconditioned taste is how the
ghost-CTA shipped: "prominent" unspecified is a vibe; "the filled
accent button Nexus puts on its download" is checkable. Two
consistency rules the ladder must satisfy internally: decision-role
data never ranks below attribution-role data (meta that decides
"is this right for me" outranks meta that credits the uploader), and
no two elements may share a rank unless the spec defends their
co-salience. The review artifact's ## Emphasis audit compares painted
hierarchy to this ladder>
|| element | rank | treatment the rank buys | treatment's referent |
||---|---|---|---|
| <each> | | | <rank 1–2 required: which exemplar element supplies the shape> |

## Registration
<required when the work adds or extends an enumerated type — a channel
kind, permission, status, role, feature id, view mode. Enumerate every
site keyed on sibling values by search, never memory: grep the sibling
keys (typeKey, TYPE_META, case "<sibling>", KIND_ICONS, channelKind,
permission-key tables) and record the command output. "none — no new
enumerated type" is the honest entry otherwise.>
| site | file | sibling keys found | disposition |
|---|---|---|---|
| <every registration site the search returned> |

## Surfaces
<required when the work adds a surface a user can reach — every place
the feature can appear outside its own module: channel tree, headers,
settings editors, landing cards, deep-link shells, notifications,
public/share pages, mobile slots, staff views. Derive by searching the
feature's routes, channel kind, and domain nouns — the remembered list
is the hypothesis, the search output is the ledger. "none — extending
an existing surface in place" is the honest entry otherwise.>
| surface | file | who reaches it | center of gravity | disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| <each> | | | <the eye-anchor — what the page is FOR: the thread, the grid, the composer — and why the shell serves it (a context rail beside a single-thread page is the 290px-chat failure, not a feature)> | |

The `center of gravity` column is v3 (D10): the page names its eye-anchor — the
one thing it exists to show — and the shell budget must serve it. A
uniform 3-column template applied to a single-thread surface starves
the conversation to keep the rail; per-page gravity is how the
template-sameness failure gets caught at spec time.

**Page surfaces re-run the derivation method (v3 — the plan-
completeness arm).** A row naming a page (`file: *.html`, a route, a
`build` disposition) is a surface *output* — it says the page exists,
never what it contains. Every such row owes a `### <surface>` block
below this table that re-runs the derivation in miniature: ≥2 member
questions in the member's own words — the question-space a member
brings *to this page* — each carrying `→ <named element(s)>` or
`omitted — <defended reason>`. The element names on the right of each
`→` are the page's declared affordance set: they land in `## Inventory`
rows, they are owed a verdict row in the build's element ledger, and
the review corpus must name them. `DECLARED: <surface> derives at
parent level — <why>` is the escape for a surface that genuinely adds
no question-space. This is the embedded-surfaces doctrine applied to
pages: a page is a design unit, and a page whose contents come from
recall instead of questions ships the generic prior — the "no sort, no
save" gap is recall wearing a plan's name.

## Authored vs inherited
<for every mechanism borrowed from the exemplar, name the expressions
this surface authors DIFFERENTLY — its own identity marks, control
shapes, copy register. ≥2 authored expressions per surface region, or
a defended clone line. A spec where every row is inherited produced
the exemplar wearing a costume — resemblance is a delta class>
| region | mechanism inherited | expression authored |
|---|---|---|

## Delta
<required whenever ## Directions exists on a restyle/redesign ask —
the question "what VISIBLY changes" answered as rows, not assumed.
Every surface or surface-group gets one row classed by what moves:
[structure] = composition — regions added/removed/resized, hierarchy
reordered, chrome re-arranged, layout skeleton changed, elements
added or removed. [surface] = tokens on unchanged composition —
color/type/spacing/radius/shadow on the same skeleton. [content] =
copy/data changes. A restyle delta that is ALL [surface] rows is a
re-skin plan: the shipped page will read as the same design re-tinted
because that is what was planned. Either name the structural move or
defend `surface-only — <why>` (the composition was already right;
the gap was execution). The `keeps:` line names each chassis
component that survives — layout skeleton / palette family / nav
architecture / density class / IA — a keep not written is a keep
never decided. `before:` cites the baseline captures the first-render
eyes pass will grade the distance from. A fresh build writes one row:
`new-build — all surfaces new`.>
| surface | class | what visibly changes | before: |
