## Depth
<only when the spec sits below the top level — a spec that expands a
parent element is a level-N spec. `depth: N` (parent+1) plus the
per-level ledger: `| level | surface/component | spec-doc |
loop-artifact | state |`, one row per level traversed. Depth 1 needs
no ## Depth at all.>

**Recursion: interactive children get their own rows.** A control or
container that holds interactive children unpacks them — the modal's
fields, the field's filter selector, the selector's options each get
rows (nested under the parent or in a sub-table keyed to it).
Termination: the atomic leaf is a single control, label, icon, or
affordance — static chrome does not recurse (a flat list of a hundred
labels is breadth-as-depth, not unpacking). A spec whose modal is one
row has a container, not a design; depth stops being the author's
invisible choice the moment the tree is declared. **Retroactive:**
revising or extending a spec re-derives ALL existing rows against the
current rules — a spec written before a new derivation requirement
is not grandfathered; the revision trigger is the catch point that
re-applies it.

**Interactive elements carry a behavior contract.** Every element that
acts — button, selector, input, link, tab, toggle, row — answers in its
row (or a `behavior:` cell): what it does (action type: navigate /
mutate / toggle / submit), invocation surface (click / Enter / Space /
command), sync or async and what the pending state shows, the error
path and retry, keyboard + a11y semantics, and the edge behaviors
(double-submit, offline, permission-denied, empty target). These are
the same terms the review's functional axis and `## Liveness` ledger
verify post-build — spec-time contract, review-time join. A button
that is spec-perfect in every design column but has no declared answer
to "what happens on second click" was styled, not designed.

**Shell chrome has a stricter reference bar.** A capture path is
not a chrome reference — a screenshot carries layout and color,
not px values, hover states, or row anatomy, so "resembles the
capture" produces ~30% fidelity and 70% prior. Chrome rows
(topbar, sidebar, member rail, logo, scrollbars) must cite the
real stylesheet/markup at `file:line` — the elements get lifted,
not approximated. Approximated chrome is fake chrome at lower
resolution.

**"Identical / match the real UI" binds the graft path, nothing
less.** When the ask is fidelity, the spec must name the method:
capture the real DOM wholesale — the *authed* state, mint the
session if needed — load the complete `<head>` stylesheet
manifest the page serves (not a hand-picked subset), and graft
the interior into real nodes. Hand-typed DOM wearing real class
names is approximation with better materials — there is no
middle option. A chrome element the dump doesn't contain is
invented: mark it wireframe; never give it a real-sounding
class name (invented classes under real names is provenance
laundering — see design-grammar banned basins).

**The vocabulary column is the lookup that makes the ban a step.**
For every element type in a graft or real-chrome surface — member
row, card, button, badge, rail panel, channel item — the spec greps
the linked stylesheets for the real class names BEFORE authoring,
and the row records `real class found — <name>` or `no real
vocabulary exists — declared invented`. `.app-members-item` was one
grep away when `.dl-memberlist` shipped invented: the ban named the
violation and nothing named the step that prevents it. The column
is the step.

**Grafted-DOM edits name the node, not the text.** A rename or
update to a lifted node touches EVERY layer of the node — visible
text, `data-*` attributes, `title`, `aria-*`, icon `data-kind`,
breadcrumbs — not just the visible label. Renaming `>announcements<`'s
text while `data-channel-name="announcements"` survived produced a
duplicate channel and a stale crumb: the graft section of the spec
lists each edited node's layers as a checklist.

**Numbers derive from data, or they are declared invented.** Any
count, total, size, or stat rendered on the surface is computed
from the dataset (`FILES.length`, a summed column) — or marked
`invented:` in its inventory row with the invented label visible.
"14 files" beside 13 rows and "1.4 GB" beside ~3.6 GB of printed
sizes is the authored-claim failure: a printed number is a claim,
and a claim that contradicts the data is a lie, not copy.

**Composition decisions are explicit.** When the spec centers a
measure ("~60%, space both sides"), it must also say which
regions share the measure vs the frame — toolbar, status bar,
head band, search. An undecided region gets the
stretch-everything default, which is how filters and the
primary CTA end up stranded at the edges, outside the reading
column's scan path. Where the eye goes is a spec decision, not
a capture-time discovery.

**The token dependency list.** The spec enumerates every
`--app-*` token authored CSS will consume (accent, borders,
surfaces, vote/component tokens), so the resolved-value check
(09-design-review Pass 0) has a list to verify. Binding a
token's name without checking its resolved value at the
element binds nothing — a bundle that redefines it on a
stronger selector produces a second accent hue on one surface,
invisible until probed.

**The affordance column binds visuals, not functions.** "Shaped
box, chevron+count" describes the good version and the bad
version identically — a text spec that can't distinguish them
binds nothing. Authored elements carry the craft decisions in
the cell: proportions, weight relationships, hover treatment,
what makes this one better than the template default. If the
cell wouldn't fail the bootstrap test's description, the element
is undesigned at spec time — say so or design it> |

**The nuance layer is where default-vs-designed actually lives.**
Coarse decisions (shape, color, spacing) produce competent; the
fine layer produces crafted. Each authored element's cell also
carries the subtlety: muted tones pulled toward the accent
family at low saturation instead of neutral grey, link vs body
tonal separation, the micro-rhythm between meta items, which
of the 2% differences were chosen and why. An element analyzed
only at coarse granularity gets the coarse default — nuance is
authored or it is absent. "Put down an input box and moved on"
happens at this layer even when the element technically has
hover states.

**The spec analyzes the surface's job before its elements.**
Before the inventory: what questions does a user bring to this
surface, in order — and which elements answer them? What should
exist and what should not — feature inclusion derived from the
user's questions and stated per element, not inherited from
what the last version had. "It was there before" is
inheritance-by-default, not a reason. And the user's first-eye
path is drawn: where the eye lands first, second, third — and
what each landing point must communicate unlabeled.

**Every region declares its communicative job before its controls.**
The surface-level job question applies per region — head, row/unit,
toolbar, rail, modal, footer: each gets one sentence of what it must
communicate ("head: you are in a maintained collection — this much
is here, this current, this required") BEFORE its controls are
listed. A region spec'd as a control list ships a control list —
present, correct, and underwhelming, because "a row of correct
controls" is not a design. The job sentence is what the controls
are composed to serve; controls without it is furniture without a
room.

