### What each section must actually contain

- **Constraint ledger.** The ask's requirements extracted verbatim —
  quoted, not paraphrased — because paraphrase is where method
  constraints die ("identical chrome" → "close-enough chrome" happens
  in the compression, not the code). Each `method` row carries its
  check form — the test that proves it held — and a row that can only
  be promised on judgment is written as an Open call instead. A spec
  without the ledger is a plan built on the compressed ask.
- **Quality contract.** Required whenever ## Directions exists — the
  measurable bar, the ≥2 distinctive moves that break the template
  read, and the ≥3 class-generic failure modes each with their
  designed-out mechanism. A spec with directions but no contract has
  decided WHAT to build without deciding what GOOD is — and
  "competent" is what ships when the bar was never written.
- **Member questions.** The surface's job stated as the member's
  questions, in the member's priority order — the only license for a
  feature to exist ("the last surface had it" is not a question).
  They are a contract: the review artifact's `## Question verdicts`
  re-asks every row against the shipped surface, and an unanswered
  question is a finding by construction. The spec that listed "what
  do I need first?" as question #1 and shipped the answer last is
  the failure this section exists to catch.
- **Domain checklist.** The artifact class's canonical features,
  derived from real exemplars of the class — not inherited from the
  previous surface. Features from the class's nature, not the last
  thing built: a file library without file formats, detail views, or
  version history is the omission-without-defense shape.
- **Emphasis ladder.** Every element ranked 1–5 with the treatment
  its rank buys — the decision that makes emphasis authored instead
  of defaulted. Written before the inventory because generation
  transcribes it: a spec with no ladder composes a surface where all
  elements sit in the same register, which reads as "nothing was
  designed." The member's primary action is rank 1 or the spec
  defends it. The review's `## Emphasis audit` compares painted
  hierarchy to this ladder — a spec can write any ladder but can't
  write one the paint contradicts.
- **Exemplar.** Name the in-repo surface this work is held to. User-
  validated surfaces are canonical anchors (the user praised it → it is
  the bar); user-criticized surfaces are negative exemplars. The
  exemplar is the *bar the surface is held to*, never the *template it
  should resemble*: borrow its mechanisms (why a shaped anchor exists),
  author this surface's expressions (what the anchor is). An artifact
  that ends up reading like the exemplar has failed its concept, not
  converged on it. When no house referent fits the surface kind, 2–3
  external exemplars of the class are required — the referent rule's
  existing doctrine (`09-design-review.mdc`), applied upstream of the
  code.
- **Exemplar decode — structure inherits, style diverges.** "Same
  quality as X" splits in two: *structural* decisions — chrome class,
  column count, section budget, density, spacing rhythm — inherit
  from the exemplar by default, and each divergence needs a named
  justification in Open calls. *Style* — identity, accent, motif,
  domain vocabulary — diverges by default so the surface is authored,
  not cloned. The failure this clause kills is the inverted decode:
  diverging on structure (novel chrome the exemplar class never uses)
  while matching on surface (recycled identity marks) — the exact
  combination that reads as costume.
- **Chrome.** The exemplar's corresponding surface is identified and
  its chrome class named; the chosen class is stated with the
  alternatives considered. A modal or sheet whose section count or
  scroll columns overflow the budget is not a styling problem — it
  is structural evidence the surface is a page, recorded in Open
  calls or the class changes. The event detail sheet shipped as a
  ten-section modal is the case this clause exists for.
- **Registration.** Search output, not assertion: the commands run
  and the sites they returned. Adding a type to N−1 of N registries
  is the "Discussion board in community settings" failure — the
  registry the list was derived from is the one that gets missed.
- **Surfaces.** The feature's reach outside its own module, derived
  by search. A surface absent from this ledger is a surface absent
  from verification — the dead public share page was a route nobody
  enumerated.
- **Concept.** One sentence deciding what kind of thing this is, written
  before any element is. A surface without a governing idea composes as
  fragments that each satisfy a checklist and read dead together —
  design-by-committee. This is the anti-fragmentation clause.
- **Grammar.** The vocabulary this surface will speak, at concrete-value
  level: "title 15px semibold --app-text, excerpt 13px --app-text-
  secondary, meta 12px --app-text-muted" — not "three voices." Extract
  it from the exemplar where the exemplar's language fits; **author it
  at the same concreteness where the surface's concept calls for a
  different language** — the bar is the level of deliberateness, not
  the exemplar's vocabulary (a departure is an Open-call entry, not
  silence). A bulleted summary is a lossy compression; the compression
  is where first-pass quality leaks.
- **Inventory.** The complete element list — every control, label,
  state, empty case — with each decision column filled **and each
  element interrogated**: why it sits where it does (placement vs
  neighbors), what measure it carries (a number or token vs the
  exemplar's equivalent — never "feels right"), and what alternatives
  were weighed (≥2, or 'sole option' defended in writing). **Zero
  decisions left inside generation**: if a styling call isn't in the
  table, it gets added to the table, not improvised in the CSS. Silent
  defaulting mid-code is the actual mechanism of utility-grade output.
  **The inventory is a contract, not documentation**: every element
  in the code traces to a row, and an element that needs to exist but
  has no row gets its row written *before* the code — writing an
  un-inventoried element is a made-mid-code decision, the exact
  failure the spec exists to kill.
  The interrogation columns are the second half of that: an element
  whose only record is which primitive it uses was placed, not
  designed — "put down an input box and moved on" is what this section
  exists to kill.
- **Open calls.** Where the exemplar is silent, the choice is enumerated
  with its options and the pick is justified — a visible decision, not a
  hidden default. This is also where a genuinely novel call gets flagged
  for the user instead of smuggled in.

## The banned basins — checked at spec time

These are the attractor states lazy generation lands in. Naming them in
the spec makes them unreachable instead of merely detectable:

- flat same-weight text runs (meta-label and quoted content sharing a
  voice)
- text pretending to be a control (a label that acts like a button)
- more than one saturated accent per surface
- boxes/dividers separating what whitespace should separate
- edge-accent bars on cards, rows, nav items, or posts — a colored
  stripe along one edge is the strongest single AI-design tell,
  user-prohibited in all four authored forms (colored/≥2px
  `border-left`, left-pinned `::before` bar, stretched thin element,
  inset box-shadow stripe; RENDER-LINT flags them at write time).
  State emphasis comes from fill, weight, tags, and hierarchy
- ornamental ordinals on navigation — `01 Home · 02 Explore` numbering
  on nav links, menu items, or section chips confers a "systematic
  document" register that reads machine-stamped on any surface that is
  not a numbered document. Two unrelated builds shipped it in one
  week — a corpus prior, not a house move. Numbers-as-content (legal §
  references, wizard "step 2 of 5", a doc whose own identity is
  numbered sections) may defend with `ordinal-ok: <why>`;
  numbering-as-chrome is banned outright (RENDER-LINT flags the
  authored forms)
- statistics-as-copy ("0 comments") where an invitation belongs
  ("Be the first to comment")
- no identity element **of its own** — a surface that could belong to
  any app, or one wearing the exemplar's identity instead of its own
  (a calendar channel in the board's contour is costume, not
  convergence)
- native browser controls where the house has a primitive —
  `<select>`, default checkboxes and radios are corpus prior; the
  house ships `ui/lib`/`design/primitives` equivalents. Using the
  native control is a regression to the median, not a neutral choice
- text naming a different feature — a label, placeholder, or heading
  that says "Discussion board settings" inside the community editor
  is wrong-product text; every user-facing string gets a noun check
  against the surface it will render on
- "frame furniture" amnesty — shell chrome (topbar, sidebar, member
  rail, logo) in any render is part of the judged surface: real
  house chrome copied from real source, or the region is visibly
  labeled wireframe. There is no third option. The user judges the
  whole screenshot, and "that wasn't the surface being designed" is
  a silent scope decision
- emoji or unicode glyphs as icons where the house has icon
  primitives (`ui/design/icons.js`) — same basin class as native
  controls: corpus prior leaking into a house render
- accent budget read as a hue count — the budget is loudness, not
  uniqueness. One hue diffused across a dozen elements (tiles,
  pills, links, glows, active states) emphasizes nothing; the
  accent's job is contrast
- `var(--app-*)` fallbacks that hide an undefined token —
  `var(--app-accent, currentColor)` reads as defensive; `--app-accent`
  was never defined and the fallback produced an invisible pressed
  state. Spec'd values name a token that exists or a literal value;
  a fallback is not a definition. *Mechanically enforced:* the Stop
  gate lints touched `.css` files for `var(--app-…)` references to
  tokens declared nowhere under `app` — `EXEMPT-TOKEN:` cites
  where a genuinely external definition lives

## Composition order — layered, not monolithic

Write in this order and commit each layer before the next: **exemplar →
chrome → concept → registration/surfaces → grammar → inventory → open
calls → code**. Chrome is decided before concept details because the
class bounds everything inside it — a modal and a page do not admit the
same inventory. Registration and surface ledgers come from *search*,
never memory, and they land before composition because they bound what
the code must reach. Monolithic composition (one pass, whole surface)
is where quality diffuses — attention spreads thin and the cheap basins
win at the margins. Layered composition gives each decision the full
attention budget, the way the exemplar itself was authored. Borrow the
exemplar's motif vocabulary wherever the surfaces are isomorphic;
invention is reserved for documented open calls — invention is where
first-pass risk lives. **Adapt, never invent**: before authoring any
pattern — a control shape, row anatomy, rail card, composer — open
the real implementation and adapt it; invented CSS where a house
primitive exists is a defect by definition, not a style choice.

## The stance — method, not mysticism

The quality this rule produces is **executable method, not a trait**.
What a working designer calls taste is mostly interrogation run
systematically — and interrogation is a procedure:

- *Per element:* What is this element's job? (No answer → cut it —
  `09-design-review.mdc` § element-names-its-job.) Which voice rank
  does it own? Is it accent-eligible? What states am I missing (empty,
  loading, error, filled, hover, long content)? Does it earn its place,
  or is it furniture added to look complete?
- *Per surface:* Where does the eye land first — is that the right
  place? Does every element cohere under the concept sentence, or is it
  a committee of parts? What did I add that earns nothing?

A miss is an unmade decision or an uninterrogated element — both are
findable by method. **Never concede a "taste floor"**: claiming a
capability limit that was never derived is itself an unverified
assumption — the same failure class the verification rules ban, worn as
humility. Report the miss as an enumerated decision or an open call,
not as an apology about ceilings.

**The calibration compounds.** The method is executable now; what
reps-and-feedback builds is the calibration inside it — same as a
junior designer. Every user critique becomes permanent grammar
(`design-grammar` skill's anchoring rule — write it into the skill the
same session it lands); every defended delta sharpens the next spec.
The floor isn't fixed — it is a slope, and the slope climbs.

## Relationship to the design-review gate (09)

The two artifacts are one cycle:

- **Spec (this rule, before edits)** — what the surface will be: the
  exemplar, the concept, the grammar, the enumerated decisions.
- **Review (`09-design-review.mdc`, after edits)** — what the surface
  became: coverage, findings, and the `## Deltas` convergence pass —
  every difference vs. the exemplar enumerated bidirectionally (moves
  the exemplar makes that the artifact missed + moves the artifact made
  that the exemplar wouldn't), each remaining delta defended in writing
  or an open finding. Property-level comparison — voice ranks, accent
  budget, separation grammar — not appearance-cloning.

A spec'd change carries both artifacts: the spec is written first, the
review is written after, and the stop gate checks both.

## Exemption — honest escape, not a skip

Genuinely non-visual edits (a logic-only JS fix, a comment correction)
need no design spec: `EXEMPT-SPEC: <file or *> — <reason>` in
`.unlazy/gate-evidence/<sid>.md`, audited like every other exemption.
Exempting to skip design work on a visual change *is* the old failure,
now loud.

## Ambition floor — pricing the declared scope (A1–A8, stop-gate armed)

On a breadth-marked improve-ask ("each/every/all <surfaces>") or any corpus
carrying a ≥5-row per-surface universe table, the spec owes these — each is
stop-gate enforced under `AMBITION-FLOOR`; evidence: `.unlazy/analysis/ambition-floor-analysis.md`.

- **VERDICT-FLOOR** — no universe-table row may end in a bare adjective
  (`fine`, `strong`, `mature`, `done`…). A verdict is `improve — <change>`,
  `keep — <why ≥40 chars>`, or `blocked — <wall>`.
- **KEEP-ALIBI** — a keep/drop/set-aside reason may not rest on
  `deliberate`/`already`/`mature`/`working` alone; it must assert a bar
  property ("holds because <X>"). Prior intent is not a user bar.
- **PROMISE-PARITY** — every `## Inventory` unit id must resurface in the
  review/analysis corpus with a disposition (`shipped`/`dropped —
  <why>`/`blocked — <wall>`). An un-dispositioned promise is invisible.
- **SWING-DECLARED** — a breadth-marked spec carries a `swing:
  polish|consolidate|restructure|rebuild` header (or
  `DECLARED: swing — <why>`). The swing class is the auditable scope ceiling.
- **DIRECTION-DIVERGENCE** — ≥1 direction must vary universe *coverage*
  (per-unit wave / anatomy rebuild / chassis change), not only risk or hue
  — or `DECLARED: directions — <why>`. Same-payoff-class alternatives are
  strawmen by construction.
